    "q-121194": {
      "dom_id": "step-q121194",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-3=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{4}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{60}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{120}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={60}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={210}^{∘}$ and $x={240}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={30}^{∘},{60}^{∘},{210}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={60}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{4}={240}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=60+240=300.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
