    "q-21088": {
      "dom_id": "step-q21088",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-{60}^{∘} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & x-{60}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={30}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={150}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={90}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=90\\cdot 210=18\\,900.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
