    "q-279990": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279990",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ in **ascending order**, what are the values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}?$ Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+1)\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+3)\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions are: \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
