    "q-8397": {
      "dom_id": "step-q8397",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-8397-a",
