    "q-279860": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279860",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values? Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 4x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 4x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{48}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{48}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{48}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{48} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{48}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{48} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(24n+17)\\pi }{48} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(24n+25)\\pi }{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{48}$ and $x=\\frac{25\\pi }{48}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{48}$ (too small) and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{48}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{41\\pi }{48}$ and $x=\\frac{49\\pi }{48}$ (too big)\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{48},\\frac{17\\pi }{48},\\frac{25\\pi }{48},\\frac{41\\pi }{48}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{17\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{25\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{41\\pi }{48}\n$$",
