    "q-279935": {
      "dom_id": "step-q279935",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=0$ for $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values?\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}${}^{∘}$",
      "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-{60}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 4x-{60}^{∘} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={45}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-{60}^{∘}$ and $x=-{45}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=-{60}^{∘},-{45}^{∘},{30}^{∘},{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\nx={-60}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={-45}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={45}^{∘}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
